Collective behavior

Coordination success, communication cost, robustness to member loss

Three core measures deserve special attention.

A. Adaptation cost

C_{\text{adapt}}=\sum_{t=t_c}^{t_c+H}
\max(0,R_{\text{reference},t}-R_{\text{agent},t})

This measures the accumulated performance deficit after the world changes at time , over a fixed evaluation horizon .

Lower is better, assuming comparable objectives. We also report recovery time separately.

B. Fusion-adaptation interaction

We calculate the benefit of adaptive cognition with and without multimodal fusion:

I=(R_{FA}-R_{FF})-(R_{VA}-R_{VF})

Here, and denote fusion and vision-only perception; and the second denote adaptive and fixed cognition.

A positive value indicates that adaptive cognition gains more from fusion than from vision alone. We compare this interaction in changing versus stable worlds.

This can provide evidence of synergy, although it does not by itself establish the mechanism.

C. Generalization gap

G=R_{\text{training}}-R_{\text{held-out}}

Lower is generally better, provided held-out performance is strong.

We also report worst-decile performance so a spectacular average doesn’t conceal catastrophic failures.

Evaluation protocol

A reasonable initial research-scale design would use:

* Approximately 1,000 procedural training worlds, 200 validation worlds, and 300 held-out test worlds, with separated generation seeds and task families.
* Five independent training runs per condition as an initial replication target.
* Paired evaluation on the same held-out environments and shift schedules.
* Clustered bootstrap confidence intervals over evaluation worlds and training runs.
* Frozen experimental settings and primary metrics before the final evaluation.

Those are proposed starting parameters, not empirically determined sample-size requirements. A pilot should establish variance and computational cost before a formal power analysis.

The importance of held-out diversity is supported by work such as Procgen⁠￼ and DeepMind’s XLand research⁠￼, which specifically explore procedural environments and generalization beyond familiar tasks.

6. Experimental discovery engine

This is where we move beyond a conventional benchmark.

The Pantheon Observatory examines unusual agent behavior and proposes follow-up experiments automatically.

Discovery candidate: Sensor substitution

Observation: An agent maintains navigation performance when its visual channel deteriorates.

Possible explanations

The agent may be using acoustic structure, accumulated spatial memory, a different environmental cue, or an unintended simulator shortcut.

Automatic falsification experiments

Replay identical scenarios with acoustic input removed, memory reset, or the suspected cue randomized. Then test new environments with different geometry.

Potential discovery

A transferable mechanism for uncertainty-aware sensory substitution—but only if controlled interventions support that conclusion.

This creates a scientific progression:

Anomaly → Hypotheses → Controlled interventions → Replication → Transfer → Preserved knowledge.

A genuinely novel sensory representation would need to survive tests against leakage, misleading correlations, and privileged simulator information.

The Observatory should preserve failures alongside successes, including the conditions under which an apparently powerful behavior breaks.

7. Possible emergent discoveries

These are hypotheses worth searching for, not claimed findings.

Discovery candidate	What we might observe	What would validate it
Sensory substitution	One modality takes over when another fails	Repeatable performance under targeted ablations
Confidence-guided perception	Agents actively seek evidence rather than passively receive it	Improved outcomes after accounting for information costs
Emergent synthetic sense	An internal representation predicts hidden environmental structure	Causal predictive value on novel environments
Collective perception	Multiple limited agents construct a better shared model	Improvement beyond independent-agent baselines
Adaptive mindset switching	Agents change exploration, deliberation, or cooperation modes appropriately	Better performance across independently held-out shift types
New affordance discovery	Agents uncover interactions not explicitly taught	Reproduction under alternative geometries and world rules
Minimal sufficient perception	A compact representation outperforms raw data of much greater size	Equal-cost transfer and robustness tests
Learning-to-learn	Agents become quicker at adapting to successive unfamiliar worlds	Improving adaptation curves without task leakage

One particularly interesting possibility is that the best synthetic sense isn’t a new physical signal at all.

It may be a new representation that fuses familiar signals into something humans cannot intuitively reconstruct from the raw data.

8. Human sensory backfill: Project ARGUS

This directly extends your idea of reverse-engineering machine-discovered senses into human tools.

Machine-discovered latent representation

A useful pattern consistently appears in agent cognition

Identify the underlying variable

Determine whether the representation encodes measurable physics, uncertainty, geometry, history, or a combination of known signals.

Build a translation layer

Convert it into a visual, acoustic, or haptic signal that humans can interpret.

Prototype a human interface

AR representation

Haptic sensory translation

Human validation

Randomize participants across unaided, conventional-display, and synthetic-sense conditions. Test whether the interface improves performance, situation awareness, response time, and workload on unfamiliar tasks.

One candidate is uncertainty vision: rather than showing an operator only what the system believes is present, display which portions of the environment have weak evidence, stale observations, or conflicting sensors.

Another is affordance vision, where geometry and object properties are translated into discoverable, physically supported interactions.

We would test these first in virtual search-and-rescue and navigation tasks, before considering any higher-stakes applications.

Success means that people actually perform better—not merely that the interface looks sophisticated.

9. Practical implementation stack

I’d avoid beginning inside an existing commercial battle royale. Its underlying physics, sensors, and agent interfaces may not expose the controls required for a rigorous experiment.

Instead, build the experimental core independently and connect it to game environments later.

Component	Candidate implementation
Multi-agent environment API	PettingZoo Parallel API
Configurable 3D embodied simulation	Habitat-Sim / Habitat-Lab
Adaptive agent cognition	Recurrent world models, reinforcement learning, belief-state estimation, meta-control
Perception	Dedicated modality encoders, temporal alignment, reliability-aware fusion
Experiment orchestration	Seeded scenario generator, condition registry, reproducible job runner
Scientific analysis	Structured event logs, statistical testing, counterfactual replay
Human interface	Game-engine HUD initially; AR and haptics subsequently

PettingZoo provides standard multi-agent interfaces, including simultaneous-action environments. Habitat supports configurable embodied agents, sensors, and navigation/manipulation tasks. Combining them into CHIMERA would require our own integration layer, not merely turning on a setting.

10. Build sequence and acceptance gates

* CHIMERA-0: Establish the laboratory
    Build a small procedural navigation-and-object-interaction world with reproducible scenarios and event logging.
    Exit gate: Identical seeds reproduce environmental states, interventions, and replayable decisions.
* CHIMERA-1: Sensory fusion
    Integrate RGB, depth, acoustic, inertial, and synthetic thermal inputs. Establish single-modality and matched fusion baselines.
    Exit gate: Sensor contributions and missing-modality effects can be individually measured.
* CHIMERA-2: Adaptive intelligence
    Add world-model updates, regime-change inference, persistent memory, and meta-control.
    Exit gate: Evaluate recovery following unseen changes, with fixed-policy comparisons.
* CHIMERA-3: Recursive discovery
    Enable the Observatory to identify anomalous behaviors, propose ablations, and compile validated transferable operators.
    Exit gate: At least one automatically proposed hypothesis survives independent replication.
* CHIMERA-4: ARGUS translation
    Translate a validated machine representation into a human-facing interface and conduct a controlled usability/performance study.
    Exit gate: Demonstrate measurable benefit against an appropriate conventional-interface baseline.

11. What Pantheon should preserve

Every meaningful discovery becomes a versioned, compressed knowledge object rather than an isolated anecdote.

Doctrine Genome — Proposed schema

Copy

Each discovery retains its hypothesis, experimental conditions, observed change, supporting evidence, counterexamples, transfer potential, and exact reconstruction instructions.

Lifecycle: Proposed → Reproduced → Causally supported → Transferred → Human validated

The most important constraint is that the system must retain enough evidence to distinguish what was discovered from what was inferred or merely imagined. The compressed semantic description is an index into the finding, not a substitute for its experimental record.

The next recursive level

Once CHIMERA works, we can expand the experimental search space across agent morphologies, additional senses, individual versus collective cognition, communication protocols, social structures, new environmental physics, and increasingly abstract domains.

We can also allow a separate experiment-design agent to optimize which questions should be investigated next, rewarding information gain and reproducible discoveries rather than simply producing impressive outcomes.

That creates three interacting evolutionary processes:

* Agent evolution: Which cognitive and sensory architectures perform best?
* Environment evolution: Which challenges reveal meaningful differences in capability?
* Scientific-method evolution: Which experiment designs most efficiently uncover reliable, transferable knowledge?

The ultimate deliverable is not one dominant bot. It’s a growing collection of experimentally validated principles that can be instantiated across games, robotics, interfaces, and other domains.

My recommended first experiment is sensor failure under an unseen environmental change. It is relatively easy to isolate, has clear quantitative outcomes, exercises both multimodal fusion and adaptive cognition, and offers a credible route to developing a human-facing uncertainty or sensory-substitution interface.

If we can demonstrate that loop end to end—discovery, falsification, reconstruction, transfer, and human augmentation—we’ve established the essential architecture for the larger Pantheon Foundry.